  - retrieval context: ['This certification requires holders to complete 45 continuing education credits every three years and attend an international conference. Since I later transitioned to the field of industrial design, I was unable to maintain this certification. Continuing education credits can be earned by attending lectures or academic conferences, but the associated costs must be covered by the individual or their company.\n\nBICSI has published a series of professional books, including references like the BICSI Guide, covering areas such as low-voltage communications, fire alarm systems, security systems, and communication systems. The organization has also established many standards in the field of communications. These publications (both print and digital versions) serve as important reference materials for low-voltage engineers.', 'Now, do all these apply to us? Not necessarily. For electrical construction and installation, many of these standards are more relevant to IT professionals. Canada has these two standards, but their applicability varies.\n\n说话人1: 00:09:58 - 00:10:29\n\nNot commonly used. In fact, apart from such industry standards, local communication providers also have some special requirements. These are the specifications we need to reference or meet in our designs, just like local computer access points. Additionally, there’s the BICSI Design Guide, which serves as its design guideline. This communication system exists within our network community.\n\n说话人1: 00:10:30 - 00:12:27', '说话人1: 00:06:56 - 00:07:55\n\nActually, for these communication standards, Canada basically adopts those from the United States, as it doesn’t have its own communication standards. Primarily, organizations like TIA, EIA, and BICSI are all American. The main standards come from TIA and EIA, while BICSI offers many guidance books—like the set of books I mentioned earlier. Additionally, BICSI also develops many Telecommunications standards, often collaborating with